    {
      "n": 4,
      "title": "Real-World Applications & Synthesis",
      "competency": "STEM_BIO11/12-IIa-j-12",
      "content": "<h3>🎯 Learning Objectives</h3><ul><li>Synthesize knowledge of photosynthesis and respiration.</li><li>Analyze real-world applications.</li><li>Compare fermentation and aerobic respiration strategies.</li><li>Evaluate biological trade-offs.</li></ul><h3>🔑 Key Concepts</h3><ul><li><strong>Trade-offs</strong> — speed vs. yield.</li><li><strong>Applications</strong> — medicine, industry, sports.</li><li><strong>Synthesis</strong> — energy flow across life.</li></ul><h3>📚 Lesson Content</h3><h4>A. Real-World Applications</h4><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Application</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Food industry</td><td>Fermentation for bread, beer, cheese, yogurt</td></tr><tr><td>Medicine</td><td>Cancer cells use aerobic glycolysis (Warburg effect)</td></tr><tr><td>Sports science</td><td>Training to increase mitochondria for endurance</td></tr><tr><td>Biofuels</td><td>Ethanol from yeast fermentation</td></tr><tr><td>Agriculture</td><td>Photosynthesis for crop yield</td></tr></tbody></table><h4>B. Photosynthesis + Respiration = Life's Energy Cycle</h4><ul><li>Photosynthesis converts light → chemical energy (glucose)</li><li>Respiration converts chemical energy → ATP</li><li>Products of one are reactants of the other</li></ul><h4>C. Key Trade-offs</h4><ul><li>Fermentation: fast but low yield</li><li>Aerobic: slower but high yield</li><li>Cells use both depending on conditions</li></ul>",
